  "body": "## Vulnerability details\n\n### Root Cause\n\nIn `previewWithdraw` ([ProgressiveCurve.sol L153](https://github.com/code-423n4/2026-03-intuition/blob/main/src/protocol/curves/ProgressiveCurve.sol#L153)), `deduct` is computed with ceiling division (`PCMath.divUp`), while `PCMath.square(s)` uses floor rounding (`mul(x, x)`):\n\n```solidity\nUD60x18 s = wrap(totalShares);\nUD60x18 deduct = PCMath.divUp(wrap(assets), HALF_SLOPE);  // ceil\nUD60x18 inner = sub(PCMath.square(s), deduct);              // floor - ceil -> underflow\n```\n\nWhen `assets == totalAssets` (full withdrawal), the relationship `totalAssets ≈ HALF_SLOPE * totalShares²` means `deduct = ceil(totalAssets / HALF_SLOPE)` can exceed `floor(totalShares²)` by 1 ULP, causing the `sub()` to underflow and revert.\n\nThis was partially addressed in PR #136 which fixed the analogous rounding issue in `_convertToAssets` (line 240) by using consistent floor rounding. However, `previewWithdraw` was missed, it still uses `divUp`.\n\n### Impact\n\n`previewWithdraw` reverts with a UD60x18 underflow for the vast majority of deposit amounts. Any external integration, UI, or on-chain contract calling `BondingCurveRegistry.previewWithdraw` (which delegates to `ProgressiveCurve.previewWithdraw`) will encounter an unexpected revert when querying a full withdrawal. This breaks the public API contract where `previewWithdraw(totalAssets, totalAssets, totalShares)` should return `totalShares` for a full withdrawal, and never revert for valid inputs (`assets <= totalAssets`).\n\n`OffsetProgressiveCurve` shares the same root cause in its `previewWithdraw` function at the same line range.\n\nNote that the actual `redeem` path via `_convertToAssets`/`previewRedeem` is NOT affected, the protocol's core deposit/redeem flow works correctly. Only the `previewWithdraw` view function is broken.\n\n### Recommended Mitigation\n\nUse floor division in `previewWithdraw`, consistent with the fix already applied to `_convertToAssets` in PR #136:\n\n```diff\n  UD60x18 s = wrap(totalShares);\n- UD60x18 deduct = PCMath.divUp(wrap(assets), HALF_SLOPE);\n+ UD60x18 deduct = div(wrap(assets), HALF_SLOPE);\n\n  UD60x18 inner = sub(PCMath.square(s), deduct);\n```\n\nApply the same fix to `OffsetProgressiveCurve.previewWithdraw`",
  "proof_of_concept": "## Proof of Concept\n\nAdd to `tests/PoCCore.t.sol` and run with `forge test --match-test test_progressiveCurve_previewWithdraw_reverts_full_withdrawal`:\n\n```solidity\nfunction test_progressiveCurve_previewWithdraw_reverts_full_withdrawal() external {\n    uint256[6] memory testAmounts = [\n        uint256(2e18),\n        uint256(3e18),\n        uint256(5e18),\n        uint256(6e18),\n        uint256(7e18),\n        uint256(1e16)\n    ];\n\n    uint256 revertCount = 0;\n\n    for (uint256 i = 0; i < testAmounts.length; i++) {\n        uint256 depositAssets = testAmounts[i];\n        uint256 totalShares = progressiveCurve.previewDeposit(depositAssets, 0, 0);\n\n        uint256 redeemAssets = progressiveCurve.previewRedeem(totalShares, totalShares, depositAssets);\n        assertTrue(redeemAssets <= depositAssets);\n\n        try progressiveCurve.previewWithdraw(depositAssets, depositAssets, totalShares) {}\n        catch { revertCount++; }\n    }\n\n    assertTrue(revertCount >= 4, \"previewWithdraw should revert for most non-perfect-square amounts\");\n\n    uint256 perfectSquareAmount = 4e18;\n    uint256 perfectShares = progressiveCurve.previewDeposit(perfectSquareAmount, 0, 0);\n    progressiveCurve.previewWithdraw(perfectSquareAmount, perfectSquareAmount, perfectShares);\n}\n```\n\nThe test shows 5 out of 6 amounts cause reverts. Only perfect-square amounts (like `4e18`, where `sqrt(4) = 2`) work, confirming the root cause.",
